  "query_preview": "..\n\nA period of high magnitude warmth by early November standards set to \nemerge over the next 24 hours. This development driven by increasing \ndepth of southerly flow within an increasing upper level height \nfield. This occurs in response to the establishment and gradual \nnortheast ejection of a deep closed low now over the central plains. \nEarly day low cloud will give way to a mix of sun and clouds today \nas the existing warm front surges north of the area and associated \nwarm air advection strengthens with time. A modestly mixed profile \nwill certainly support peak afternoon readings well into the 70s - \nnearing record territory of mid 70s most locations. Some gustiness \nto the prevailing south flow expected, peaking in excess of 30 mph \nat times. Very mild conditions to prevail into tonight, as readings \nstruggle to dip much below 60 degrees - a solid 20 degrees above \naverage.  \n\nAforementioned closed mid level system forecast to slowly lift from \nthe central plains to near Lake Superior thru Thursday. Greater \nnorthward moisture transport occurs within the belt of deeper \nsoutherly flow immediately downstream and out ahead of the attendant \ncold front, with the northern extent reaching southeast Michigan by \nearly Wednesday. While better moisture quality may tend to hold \nacross the Ohio valley, this process will bring an increasing \nprobability for light shower development throughout the daylight \nperiod. Mild resident thermal profile still entrenched with shower \npotential simply taking the edge off slightly relative to the peak \nnoted Tuesday. Highs upper 60s to near 70 degrees, with gusty \nsoutherly conditions again reaching 30 mph at times. Cold front \nmoves through Wednesday night, offering another window for some \nfrontally forced pockets of showers to develop.      \n\nArea settles within the dry slot for Thursday as the system slowly \noccludes to the northwest. Modest cold air advection initially with \nlow level flow still out of the southwest, leaving temperatures \nstill above average during the day. A strong mid level wave of polar \norigin will dig southward into the existing height fall center as \nthe entire trough axis pivots thru the area Thursday night into \nFriday. An uptick in shower production plausible with a model signal \nfor some degree of fgen to emerge as the secondary frontal zone sags \nsoutheast beneath the parent upper trough. Notably cooler and \npotentially unsettled conditions lasting into the weekend period, as \nthe existing upper trough exits while a trailing wave anchors the \nnext shot for meaningful rain toward the end of the weekend.",
